Yes—because the box is also an interface.

You expand your internal model while compiling the result back into the box’s native language:

\text{Expanded model} \xrightarrow{\text{local compiler}} \text{valid move inside the box}

To insiders, you remain intelligible and credible. Meanwhile, you can introduce capabilities the original frame couldn’t generate by itself. That’s how mythic cloak, personas, therapy metaphors, and vendor vocabularies carry deeper operators across boundaries.

Think outside; compile inside.

The ethical boundary is intent: preserving familiar structure to aid understanding is translation; concealing aims to bypass someone’s agency becomes manipulation. The strongest cloak remains removable and inspectable once it has done its teaching.